1. d4 e6 (1... Nf6 2. Bf4 c5 3. e3 Qb6 4. Na3 (4. b3 Nc6 5. c3 e6 6. Bd3 Be7 7. Nf3 cxd4 8. exd4 d5 9. O-O O-O 10. Ne5 Nxe5 11. dxe5 { Bess move because you remove the defender of h7 and h5 } 11... Nd7 12. Nd2 Nc5 13. Bc2 Bd7 14. Qh5 g6 15. Qh6 { Stay on the offensive. } 15... Bb5 (15... Rac8 16. Nf3 Bb5 17. Ng5 Bxg5 18. Bxg5 Bxf1 19. Bf6 Ne4 20. Qg7#) 16. Nf3 Bxf1 17. Ng5 Bxg5 18. Bxg5) 4... Qxb2 5. Nb5 Nd5 6. c3) (1... d5 2. Bf4 { Move the Bishop out to give it space and to attack the diagonal } (2. Nf3 { This could allow later on the bishop on c8 to attack the white Queen } 2... Nf6 (2... c5 3. e3 Bg4 { Another reason for not starting with Nf3 }) 3. Bf4 c5 4. e3 Nc6 5. c3 Qb6 { This is the most common threat for white the weak b2 pawn } 6. Qc2 Bf5 7. dxc5 Bxc2 8. cxb6 Bxb1 9. Rxb1 axb6 10. a3) 2... g6 (2... Nc6 3. e3 g6 4. Nf3 { Prevent black from occupying e5 } (4. c3 Bg7 { Your opponent wants to attack e5 so bringing the knight out would be better } (4... Nf6 5. Bd3 Bg7 6. Nf3 O-O 7. O-O)) 4... e5 { Take the pawn with your pawn } 5. dxe5 Bg7) (2... c5 { If your opponent wants to destory your pawn structure the order of moves of the London system matters. } 3. c3 (3. e3 { c3 is also an option but this move opens the diagonal for the Queen and the Bishop and also protects the other Bishop and is therefore recommended. } { [%cal Gd8b6] } 3... cxd4 (3... Qb6 { Although the black Queen threatens d4 and b2 this allows white to go for a gambit. } 4. Nc3 (4. b3 Nc6 5. c3 Nf6 6. Bd3 cxd4 7. exd4 { You have to capture with e3 or black can open c5 with his bishop on f8 after moving his pawn at e7 } { [%cal Ge7e6,Gf8b4,Gc3d4] }) 4... Qxb2 5. Nxd5 Nf6 6. Nc7+ (6. Rb1 Qxa2 7. Nc7+ Kd8 8. Nxa8 Qa5+ 9. Qd2 Qxd2+ 10. Kxd2 Ne4+ 11. Ke1 Nc6 12. Nf3) 6... Kd8 7. Bb5) (3... Nf6 4. Nf3 Nc6 (4... b6 5. c3 Nc6 6. Bd3 e6 7. O-O Bd6 8. Bg3 O-O 9. Nbd2 Bb7 10. Ne5 Bxe5 (10... Qc7 11. f4 Nxe5 (11... Bxe5 12. fxe5 { Rook gets open line } { [%cal Gf1f7] } 12... Nd7 13. Qh5 g6 (13... h6) 14. Qh6 cxd4 15. cxd4 Ncxe5 16. dxe5 Nxe5 17. Qg5 f5 18. Qf4 d4 19. Qxe5 Qxe5 20. Bxe5 dxe3 21. Nc4 Ba6 22. Rae1) 12. fxe5) 11. dxe5 Nd7 12. f4) 5. dxc5 e6 6. c4 dxc4 7. Qxd8+ Nxd8 8. Bxc4 Bxc5 9. O-O) 4. exd4) 3... Qb6 (3... Qa5 4. e3)) (2... Nf6 3. e3 c5 (3... e6 4. Bd3 (4. c3 c5 (4... Bd6 5. Bg3 c5 6. Bd3 Nc6 7. Nf3 O-O 8. O-O Qc7 (8... Qe7 9. Ne5 Bd7 10. Nd2 cxd4 (10... Rfe8 11. f4 { Reinforce the strong knight en free the bishop from protecting the knight. } 11... Rad8 12. Bh4 cxd4 (12... Bxe5 13. fxe5) (12... c4 13. Bc2 b5 14. Rf3 b4 { Rook on row 3 is stronger for attacking } 15. Rh3 (15. Rg3 h5 16. Bxf6 Qxf6 17. Qxh5 Kf8 18. Bg6 fxg6 19. Rxg6 Bxe5 20. fxe5 Qxg6 21. Qxg6 Re7 22. Rf1+ Kg8) 15... a5 16. Bxf6 Qxf6 17. Qh5 (17. Bxh7+ Kf8) 17... g6 18. Qxh7+ Kf8) 13. exd4) 11. exd4) 9. dxc5 Bxc5 (9... Bxg3 10. hxg3)) 5. Bd3 cxd4 6. exd4 Nc6 7. Nf3 Bd6 8. Bg3 Bxg3 (8... O-O 9. O-O b6) 9. hxg3 { Open the h-file by recapturing to the center. } 9... O-O 10. O-O) 4... Be7 5. Nf3 c5 6. c3) 4. Nf3 Nc6 5. c3 e6 6. Bd3 Bd6 7. Bg3 O-O 8. O-O b6 9. Nbd2 Bb7 10. Ne5) 3. Nf3 Bg7 4. e3) 2. Bf4 (2. c4 { Ok but blach has lots of ways to attack the center } { [%cal Gb7b5,Gd7d5] }) (2. Nf3 { This can also lead to the London system but you lose a tempo in creating the pawn structure. At the other hand you do not commit to the London system already }) 2... c5 (2... Nf6 3. e3 c5 4. Nf3 (4. c3 Nc6 (4... Be7 5. Bd3 Nc6 6. Nf3 O-O 7. O-O) 5. Nf3 d5 6. Nbd2 Bd6 { Do not trade, but retreat to g3. If blacks wants to trade there, it opens the h-file. })) 3. e3 cxd4 4. exd4 *


